## Reflection
This was my first time working with Tsurugi, and I can see why having a DFIR-focused environment would be useful. Hindsight made it fairly 
easy to take a Chrome history database and turn it into something I could actually investigate.

I also got some unexpected Linux troubleshooting practice after misspelling a directory name and spending much longer than I would like to admit figuring 
out why my commands weren't working.

My biggest takeaway from this lab was that finding evidence is only half of the investigation. I could determine which sites were visited, when they were visited, 
and the order of the activity, but I did not have enough evidence to determine the user's intent. I need to be able to separate what the evidence proves from what I think may have happened.

## More to Explore
- Analyze a larger and more complete browser history dataset
- Examine additional browser artifacts such as cookies, downloads, searches, and cached files
- Explore more of Tsurugi's built-in forensic tools
- Analyze file system and system log artifacts
- Correlate multiple forensic artifacts to build a more complete timeline
- Practice investigating a larger scenario where the goal is to determine what happened rather than analyzing a single artifact

